{
  "summary": "The seven prior rounds have converged on a sensible three-tier extraction strategy but share a collective blind spot that undermines the entire plan's premise: they treat the refactor as a single coherent project when it is actually three unrelated problems with different risk profiles, timelines, and payoffs — and bundling them guarantees the lowest-risk work gets blocked by analysis paralysis over the hardest parts. Specifically, Tier 1 (pure helper extraction) and Tier 3 (@work-decorated orchestration methods) have almost nothing in common — one is a mechanical file-move that any developer could do in an hour, the other is a genuine architectural challenge requiring decisions about thread-safety, state ownership, and Textual's worker lifecycle. Yet every round treats them as phases of one plan, which means the trivial Tier 1 work has been sitting undone for seven rounds of debate about Tier 3. The correct approach is to decouple them entirely: execute Tier 1 immediately as a standalone PR with no design discussion needed, then separately evaluate whether Tier 2/3 are even worth doing given the actual bug rate and feature velocity in those code paths. The second underexamined weakness is that every round assumes the refactor's value comes from maintainability, but nobody has quantified the actual pain. If tui.py changes infrequently and is primarily authored by one or two people, the maintenance cost of a monolith is low and the refactor's ROI is negative — the real cost is not 'hard to navigate' (any editor can jump to definition) but 'hard to test,' and the prior rounds' own analysis shows that Textual's @work and reactive systems make extracted code equally hard to test without a full app harness. A characterization test suite for the current monolith would deliver 80% of the safety benefit at 20% of the effort, without any structural changes. Third, the repeated proposals for a narrow Protocol/ActionContext interface (rounds 3, 5, 6, 7) sound clean but ignore a practical reality visible in the actual code: the action methods don't just read state — they mutate it in complex, order-dependent ways (setting _plan_running, updating cooldown dicts, discarding from queued sets, releasing locks in finally blocks at lines 3944-3951). A Protocol exposing only 8 attributes would either need to grow to cover all these mutations (becoming PipelineApp in disguise) or the extracted functions would need to return complex result objects that PipelineApp interprets (adding indirection without reducing coupling). The honest assessment is that _run_plan_async and _run_implement_async are inherently coupled to PipelineApp's mutable state and the cleanest extraction target is not 'move them to another file' but 'simplify them in place' by extracting the duplicated lock-acquire/status-reset/error-handling boilerplate into a context manager, which would cut each method by 30-40% without any file-splitting."
}
